1
00:00:03,327 --> 00:00:14,609
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to talk about Brahma-vihāra again, and a few other things that seem to be connected.

2
00:00:16,229 --> 00:00:31,432
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we've been discussing how the Brahma-vihāra practice is the practice of the peaceful life, the life of non-conflict.

3
00:00:34,825 --> 00:00:40,547
[SPEAKER_01]: And, so to say, just say with me, I've got something to say.

4
00:00:42,828 --> 00:00:50,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my idea is that these four aspects of Brahma-vihāra is one mind.

5
00:00:50,230 --> 00:00:52,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Metta, Karuṇa, Mudita, Upeka.

6
00:00:54,671 --> 00:01:02,614
[SPEAKER_01]: These four aspects need to be discerned one by one.

7
00:01:04,415 --> 00:01:16,183
[SPEAKER_01]: And when matters discerned and made steady, one can then discern Karuṇa, one can then discern Udita, and then one can then discern Rūpaka.

8
00:01:16,684 --> 00:01:17,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it's fulfilled.

9
00:01:17,885 --> 00:01:22,908
[SPEAKER_01]: That peaceful mind, that peaceful life is being lived and fulfilled.

10
00:01:22,928 --> 00:01:28,032
[SPEAKER_00]: So, more accurately then, instead of saying one by one, it's one within the other.

11
00:01:29,292 --> 00:01:31,193
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the development of that peaceful mind.

12
00:01:31,433 --> 00:01:36,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by understanding what metta is, you understand the principle of all four Brahma-vihāras.

13
00:01:37,836 --> 00:01:42,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, within that understanding of metta, development of metta, you can develop karuṇa.

14
00:01:42,157 --> 00:01:48,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Within development of metta and karuṇa, you then get to develop mudita, which then results in development of kūpeka.

15
00:01:50,101 --> 00:01:53,222
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't abandon metta in order to develop karuṇa.

16
00:01:53,622 --> 00:01:56,003
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't abandon metta and karuṇa in order to develop mudita.

17
00:01:56,515 --> 00:02:05,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Upeka encompasses them all, but in order to arrive at it, you have to follow through them, so to speak.

18
00:02:05,581 --> 00:02:10,724
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like, oh, I do metta, I feel accomplished, now I find out what karuṇa is, I feel accomplished.

19
00:02:11,124 --> 00:02:14,346
[SPEAKER_00]: If you keep doing metta, you will arrive at upeka, basically.

20
00:02:16,768 --> 00:02:20,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, if you keep following the principle of the first jhāna, you will arrive at the fourth.

21
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:26,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the idea is peacefulness, non-conflict.

22
00:02:28,645 --> 00:02:34,970
[SPEAKER_00]: On that general level, level of what we refer to as a framework, not an individual level of the content of your experience.

23
00:02:35,931 --> 00:02:45,298
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of metta, the Buddha seemed to mean non-ill-will, rather than an act of friendly activity.

24
00:02:45,318 --> 00:02:46,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course, of course.

25
00:02:46,819 --> 00:02:48,281
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a non-ill-will.

26
00:02:48,601 --> 00:02:49,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Framework of non-ill-will.

27
00:02:50,880 --> 00:03:09,194
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind of non-ill-will means, even when on a particular level, in terms of the content of your current sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, there is something disagreeable, you will not have ill-will towards it, because you prioritize protecting the mind of non-ill-will, regardless of what content that mind is experiencing.

28
00:03:09,754 --> 00:03:16,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of whenever you experience something unpleasant, you have non-ill-will towards it, you have non-resistance towards it.

29
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:16,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

30
00:03:17,856 --> 00:03:18,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

31
00:03:18,657 --> 00:03:20,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-ill-will means non-resistance.

32
00:03:20,338 --> 00:03:32,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, which is different from a, from what I usually read as a practice for metta is, you sort of, you have metta for things you like.

33
00:03:33,606 --> 00:03:34,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

34
00:03:34,387 --> 00:03:35,448
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you generate love.

35
00:03:35,788 --> 00:03:36,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

36
00:03:36,248 --> 00:03:41,972
[SPEAKER_01]: And that feeling of love, yeah, of pleasure towards something you like, that's what you keep going.

37
00:03:42,132 --> 00:03:44,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's not what the suttas talk, mean by, yeah.

38
00:03:44,453 --> 00:03:46,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's usually the contemporary notions of

39
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:49,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, spirituality and everything else.

40
00:03:49,501 --> 00:03:50,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's more palpable.

41
00:03:51,582 --> 00:03:55,283
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more palpable for people to sort of... And it's nice.

42
00:03:56,483 --> 00:04:06,827
[SPEAKER_00]: It's better to think in that kind of loving-kindness manner towards people, than to dislike them or ignore them.

43
00:04:07,788 --> 00:04:10,148
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a mistake to regard that as a practice of the dhamma.

44
00:04:10,328 --> 00:04:11,349
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem here.

45
00:04:12,065 --> 00:04:23,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, really, if you want to think positive thoughts about everyone, by all means, but don't mistake that for the practice of dhamma, because if you do, then you won't look for what the actual dhamma and metta is in the suttas.

46
00:04:24,037 --> 00:04:25,139
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the main problem.

47
00:04:25,179 --> 00:04:31,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like... saying that that's not dhamma doesn't mean you should not do it, because it's a nice... it's certainly a good way to relate with people, because...

48
00:04:33,205 --> 00:04:35,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Not hating them means they will not hate you back.

49
00:04:35,586 --> 00:04:36,427
[SPEAKER_00]: More often they're not.

50
00:04:36,507 --> 00:04:39,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just going to help you in that level of society.

51
00:04:39,349 --> 00:04:43,711
[SPEAKER_00]: But if people make that into spiritual practice, that's when they're going wrong.

52
00:04:44,131 --> 00:04:46,273
[SPEAKER_01]: But without understanding what the Buddha meant.

53
00:04:46,293 --> 00:04:47,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

54
00:04:47,313 --> 00:04:52,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you end up just practicing dhamma rooted in, like, how you feel.

55
00:04:52,716 --> 00:04:54,097
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, oh, this makes me feel good.

56
00:04:54,157 --> 00:04:55,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, that's the practice of the dhamma.

57
00:04:56,759 --> 00:04:58,740
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's more often they're not wrong.

58
00:05:00,429 --> 00:05:05,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your feelings are already affected by your own resistance towards it or indulgence towards it.

59
00:05:05,790 --> 00:05:14,472
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that's your only criteria for following what the practice of dhamma is, means you just... your criteria of what dhamma is, is your criteria of ignorance, criteria of craving.

60
00:05:17,913 --> 00:05:23,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, matter in the... what the Buddha means is non-ill will.

61
00:05:25,175 --> 00:05:25,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

62
00:05:25,895 --> 00:05:26,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Framework of non-ill will.

63
00:05:27,356 --> 00:05:28,416
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind of non-ill will.

64
00:05:29,308 --> 00:05:37,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but if it was love... See, but it's also... it's not... the reason why I keep saying it's the mind of non-ill-will, not just non-ill-will.

65
00:05:37,859 --> 00:05:41,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I might not have ill-will towards you, but I have ill-will towards him.

66
00:05:41,763 --> 00:05:44,527
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean I'm doing matter towards you because I don't hate you.

67
00:05:46,187 --> 00:05:48,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, basically, my mind is not developed in regard to matter.

68
00:05:49,189 --> 00:05:53,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if I don't hate anybody individually, that still doesn't mean you're developing matter.

69
00:05:54,133 --> 00:06:06,042
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to develop matter, you need to recognize that framework, that absence of your will, as the basis for your mind state, that you then choose to protect and maintain, regardless of who comes, basically, in your field of vision.

70
00:06:06,062 --> 00:06:07,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of the circumstance.

71
00:06:07,663 --> 00:06:11,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of the circumstance, regardless of the elements, regardless of anything you're subjected to.

72
00:06:12,607 --> 00:06:14,328
[SPEAKER_00]: You... you... because you always have a choice.

73
00:06:14,388 --> 00:06:26,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can now ignore the mind of non-ill will as a priority and I can choose to prioritize what I'm experiencing right in front of me, as in dealing with you, dealing with that, dealing with this upset, dealing with that pleasure or whatever.

74
00:06:27,637 --> 00:06:33,300
[SPEAKER_00]: And that choice basically means you abandon the framework for the sake of the content that's within that framework.

75
00:06:35,922 --> 00:06:37,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you're not developing the method.

76
00:06:38,582 --> 00:06:44,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why that sutta, which is, again, contemporarily, completely misread and mischanted.

77
00:06:44,363 --> 00:06:44,964
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember that.

78
00:06:45,384 --> 00:06:47,944
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's all due to, like, putting a different punctuation.

79
00:06:48,504 --> 00:06:57,426
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the line, we spoke about it before, um, uh, he, uh, like the famous line, where he, um, like a mother protects her child.

80
00:06:57,546 --> 00:07:03,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that line refers to that he protects his mind of non-ill will, like a mother protects her only child.

81
00:07:04,048 --> 00:07:06,729
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you think, I must regard everybody like my own children.

82
00:07:06,849 --> 00:07:08,189
[SPEAKER_00]: That's completely not what it means.

83
00:07:09,249 --> 00:07:20,273
[SPEAKER_00]: What it means that if you regard your own mind as your only child, as in, it's so important and precious to not lose sight of it, mind of non-ill will, you will automatically cherish all living beings.

84
00:07:20,873 --> 00:07:31,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't, don't need to take my word for it, you could just, like, see which one then encompasses which principle, either the one I describe or the principle of cherishing all living beings like your children.

85
00:07:32,498 --> 00:07:35,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one better corresponds to all four brahma-viharas?

86
00:07:36,299 --> 00:07:46,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one better corresponds to all other aspects of the dhamma teachings, whereby, when the mother brought the baby and left it in front of the arahant, he didn't even look at it.

87
00:07:47,364 --> 00:07:50,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And the wife realized this, he will leave this child to die.

88
00:07:50,765 --> 00:07:52,626
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how much he does not care about it.

89
00:07:53,046 --> 00:07:55,287
[SPEAKER_00]: And then she came back, picked up the child and left.

90
00:07:56,007 --> 00:07:57,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And the people reported that to the Buddha.

91
00:07:57,948 --> 00:08:00,509
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, well done, that's how every arahant and monk should act.

92
00:08:02,682 --> 00:08:06,663
[SPEAKER_00]: So, which one of all of those instances would be then encompassed?

93
00:08:06,723 --> 00:08:08,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Which principle would encompass it better?

94
00:08:08,403 --> 00:08:15,345
[SPEAKER_00]: The one I'm describing, whereby, if you protect your mind, as your only child, you automatically cannot love, nor hate.

95
00:08:15,485 --> 00:08:20,846
[SPEAKER_00]: You can automatically cannot hate, nor love, or be infatuated, or emotionally bound with the whole world.

96
00:08:21,946 --> 00:08:24,647
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's far better than loving and being emotionally bound.

97
00:08:26,067 --> 00:08:27,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the principle of loving everyone.

98
00:08:27,627 --> 00:08:30,008
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you then tie that in with being indifferent to everyone?

99
00:08:30,897 --> 00:08:33,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but indifference comes as a result of excessive love?

100
00:08:33,981 --> 00:08:34,942
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not how it works.

101
00:08:36,164 --> 00:08:38,567
[SPEAKER_00]: And you already know that, even without any insight into dhamma.

102
00:08:38,587 --> 00:08:48,058
[SPEAKER_00]: This is that love breeds... Well, love breeds infatuation, yeah, and then makes you susceptible to aversion, makes you susceptible to fear and anxiety over what you love.

103
00:08:48,947 --> 00:08:53,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now you must put... you have no say in it, yet you're dependent on it emotionally and existentially.

104
00:08:53,489 --> 00:08:56,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Someone will come up and might just try to destroy the thing that you love.

105
00:08:56,751 --> 00:08:57,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

106
00:08:57,371 --> 00:08:58,331
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what is...

107
00:08:58,351 --> 00:09:00,972
[SPEAKER_00]: So, against... Like, what did the Buddha describe to fit that?

108
00:09:01,913 --> 00:09:02,713
[SPEAKER_00]: It was centrality.

109
00:09:03,414 --> 00:09:12,858
[SPEAKER_00]: He said it's like, for the sake of enjoying the ripe mangoes, you climb the tree, and somebody else who also wants to enjoy those mangoes cannot climb the tree, but they can cut it.

110
00:09:14,198 --> 00:09:23,146
[SPEAKER_00]: And unless you come down and abandon you enjoying and eating and protecting and being infatuated with your fruit, you will suffer on account of that tree being cut and probably even die.

111
00:09:26,227 --> 00:09:26,947
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

112
00:09:27,348 --> 00:09:41,515
[SPEAKER_00]: By all means, have positive thoughts towards the whole world, but do not mistake that to be the matter that the Buddha described that would result in upekkhā and utter complete freedom from the slightest conceit and everything else and attachment to the world.

113
00:09:41,655 --> 00:09:48,078
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can't see the connection between matter and upekkhā, if you can't see that... Yeah, that means your matter needs redefining.

114
00:09:49,865 --> 00:09:59,229
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, the further development is then karuṇa, getting it more, even steadier, which is non-cruelty.

115
00:09:59,750 --> 00:10:12,035
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... which is, again, to me it sounds similar to not, you know, non-craving, in a way.

116
00:10:12,515 --> 00:10:15,517
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, non-craving is the ultimate non-cruelty.

117
00:10:16,363 --> 00:10:19,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, it's basically taking the same principle a step further now.

118
00:10:20,925 --> 00:10:28,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first you have obvious, like, you meet disagreeable, you have ill will towards it, mind of ill will.

119
00:10:30,188 --> 00:10:36,230
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop the mind of non-ill will in regard to disagreeable, but then you have that kind of still a degree of some resistance.

120
00:10:36,610 --> 00:10:42,092
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to develop the mind of that non-ill will, you would be then cultivating the non-resistance.

121
00:10:42,711 --> 00:10:45,173
[SPEAKER_00]: It then becomes like karuṇa, the next step.

122
00:10:45,493 --> 00:10:48,735
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you refine the same principle of non-resistance.

123
00:10:49,136 --> 00:10:53,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's, by the way, what's in common to metta, karuṇa, mudita and upeka.

124
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:56,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-resistance.

125
00:10:57,141 --> 00:10:58,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-resistance means no craving.

126
00:10:58,642 --> 00:10:59,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, non-conflict.

127
00:10:59,743 --> 00:11:01,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-conflict means no craving, yeah.

128
00:11:02,605 --> 00:11:03,526
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's, eh...

129
00:11:05,327 --> 00:11:05,908
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all about...

130
00:11:06,168 --> 00:11:08,970
[SPEAKER_01]: It's got to do with understanding, wisdom, discernment.

131
00:11:10,309 --> 00:11:16,852
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, for example, that other dhamma part of verse 3, it says, you recognize nobody wants to get hurt.

132
00:11:17,672 --> 00:11:18,552
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to get hurt.

133
00:11:19,333 --> 00:11:20,373
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, why?

134
00:11:20,433 --> 00:11:21,654
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't hurt anyone.

135
00:11:22,354 --> 00:11:23,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the way.

136
00:11:23,494 --> 00:11:26,155
[SPEAKER_01]: That understanding is compassion.

137
00:11:28,116 --> 00:11:28,556
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

138
00:11:28,897 --> 00:11:31,998
[SPEAKER_00]: If understanding is correct, you don't have to do the compassion.

139
00:11:33,378 --> 00:11:34,679
[SPEAKER_00]: The result is compassion.

140
00:11:37,363 --> 00:11:39,444
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's that steadying of that discernment of...

141
00:11:39,464 --> 00:11:39,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

142
00:11:40,064 --> 00:11:40,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

143
00:11:40,964 --> 00:11:45,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Not emulating the compassion by visualizing friendliness and love...

144
00:11:45,545 --> 00:11:46,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Saving someone... Yeah.

145
00:11:47,406 --> 00:11:47,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

146
00:11:47,726 --> 00:11:50,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Or dedicating your life to saving other people's lives and so on.

147
00:11:50,246 --> 00:11:55,948
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can help without whatever... any cost of your own well-being, that's great.

148
00:11:56,448 --> 00:11:58,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you want to sacrifice yourself, that's fine.

149
00:11:58,228 --> 00:11:59,249
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure you have your reasons.

150
00:11:59,789 --> 00:12:03,330
[SPEAKER_00]: But in terms of the dhamma practice, none of that would be the practice of the dhamma.

151
00:12:03,430 --> 00:12:04,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Practice of dhamma is development of the mind.

152
00:12:08,576 --> 00:12:12,399
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to whatever's felt pleasant, unpleasant.

153
00:12:12,419 --> 00:12:15,401
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's unpleasant, there's no resistance there.

154
00:12:16,321 --> 00:12:17,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

155
00:12:17,222 --> 00:12:18,863
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's pleasant, you're not trying to get it.

156
00:12:19,183 --> 00:12:21,685
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not trying to change circumstances to get it.

157
00:12:21,705 --> 00:12:31,191
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why people would want to get the things that are pleasant is because there is also a degree of resistance to the lack of that pleasure.

158
00:12:31,531 --> 00:12:33,613
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not pleasant enough and you resist that.

159
00:12:35,167 --> 00:12:36,208
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to accept that.

160
00:12:36,268 --> 00:12:37,229
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to change it.

161
00:12:37,690 --> 00:12:38,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, regardless of...

162
00:12:39,151 --> 00:12:42,974
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you develop brahma vihāras, you will be overcoming sensuality as well.

163
00:12:43,655 --> 00:12:45,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sensuality is also a form of resistance.

164
00:12:46,417 --> 00:12:52,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Just instead of resisting against the content of your experience, you're resisting towards more of it.

165
00:12:55,542 --> 00:13:06,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can... the practice of that peaceful mind, in regards to whatever is felt, that's really the practice, right there.

166
00:13:06,210 --> 00:13:06,991
[SPEAKER_01]: It covers everything.

167
00:13:07,151 --> 00:13:08,032
[SPEAKER_01]: We said that before.

168
00:13:08,132 --> 00:13:17,219
[SPEAKER_00]: If you practice brahmahāras in regard to whatever current feeling is presently enduring, you are already practicing that framework inadvertently.

169
00:13:17,479 --> 00:13:21,102
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even think about the framework in the more general picture, in the non-content.

170
00:13:21,782 --> 00:13:24,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Just have non-resistance in regard to presently enduring feeling.

171
00:13:26,642 --> 00:13:27,022
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

172
00:13:28,863 --> 00:13:34,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-resistance towards more of it, non-resistance away from it, non-resistance towards ignoring it.

173
00:13:34,786 --> 00:13:51,796
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't possibly try to hurt someone, because you don't... No, because intention to hurt, intention to engage in sensuality, intention to harm, intention to be cruel, is always universally rooted every single time, in every single being, regardless of the culture, mental faculties.

174
00:13:51,836 --> 00:13:55,838
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always rooted in them resisting whatever they're feeling at the time.

175
00:13:57,330 --> 00:13:59,412
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot... that's why it's unwholesome, inherently.

176
00:14:01,053 --> 00:14:03,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you cannot be cruel for the good reason.

177
00:14:03,555 --> 00:14:04,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

178
00:14:04,796 --> 00:14:05,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

179
00:14:06,497 --> 00:14:12,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Because intention of cruelty requires, as its necessary basis, you resisting how you feel.

180
00:14:16,425 --> 00:14:26,693
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person who has developed that much, and steadied his mind that much, that practice of peacefulness,

181
00:14:28,408 --> 00:14:39,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Regardless of what is felt, non-craving, there... it's an automatic... Contentment.

182
00:14:39,876 --> 00:14:41,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, contentment is there, automatically.

183
00:14:42,237 --> 00:14:43,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's a result of it.

184
00:14:44,399 --> 00:14:45,800
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs to also discern that.

185
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, which he would.

186
00:14:47,621 --> 00:14:53,785
[SPEAKER_00]: If he already developed that point, he would discern that he's content amidst this contenting environment.

187
00:14:54,672 --> 00:15:02,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's how Mudita described in Anguttara Nikaya, how does he get rid of discontent by practicing Mudita.

188
00:15:03,077 --> 00:15:03,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

189
00:15:03,437 --> 00:15:04,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is contentment.

190
00:15:04,918 --> 00:15:10,722
[SPEAKER_00]: See, and that's... that's... it's another step further of the same principle that you started with in regard to metta.

191
00:15:11,222 --> 00:15:13,424
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... it's even less personal.

192
00:15:14,210 --> 00:15:18,312
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now it's not even anymore about others and your involvement with others or this or that.

193
00:15:18,392 --> 00:15:21,073
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really on the level of content-discontent.

194
00:15:21,754 --> 00:15:36,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Being able to endure discontent, being able to endure elements, being able to endure sickness, being able to endure abuse, disagreement, criticism, being able to endure basically anything that you're subjected to and not lose the mind of contentment, that's mudita.

195
00:15:36,681 --> 00:15:40,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see that's already higher than just enduring non-ill will.

196
00:15:41,757 --> 00:15:45,239
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that spiritual joy, so to speak, that people...

197
00:15:45,299 --> 00:15:48,140
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how to use it, but... Yeah, yeah, because spiritual joy tends to be like...

198
00:15:48,260 --> 00:15:51,241
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a joy that's not connected with whatever's happening.

199
00:15:51,281 --> 00:15:52,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

200
00:15:52,402 --> 00:15:55,383
[SPEAKER_00]: It's truly imperturbable by any circumstances.

201
00:15:56,064 --> 00:15:59,325
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the same joy you get in jhāna, yeah.

202
00:16:00,610 --> 00:16:05,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Joy that you're secluded and unaffected, free of concern in regard to the entire world.

203
00:16:05,996 --> 00:16:07,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is a strengthening of what you're doing.

204
00:16:08,238 --> 00:16:08,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

205
00:16:08,438 --> 00:16:09,800
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only strengthen what you're doing.

206
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:15,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you could see how naturally, if you would develop that principle further, it's the utter equanimity.

207
00:16:15,846 --> 00:16:16,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Complete.

208
00:16:17,007 --> 00:16:32,985
[SPEAKER_00]: And you wouldn't, like, have to sort of, you know, do patchwork and tie loose ends to connect your love of love of metta and metta loving with utter indifference towards everything, everyone, including your own life, your own emotions, your own state of being, which arahant would be developing.

209
00:16:34,073 --> 00:16:40,337
[SPEAKER_00]: But if what we describe, you realize how metta would lead to karuṇa, which leads to, basically, contentment amidst discontent.

210
00:16:40,897 --> 00:16:45,339
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, your mind does not resist unpleasant circumstances, unpleasant elements.

211
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:46,880
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is that result?

212
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:48,041
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it culminate in?

213
00:16:48,601 --> 00:16:49,642
[SPEAKER_00]: In utter equanimity.

214
00:16:50,462 --> 00:16:51,623
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to good or bad.

215
00:16:52,944 --> 00:16:55,625
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, by all means, practice brahmavihāras correctly.

216
00:16:56,025 --> 00:16:56,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

217
00:16:57,406 --> 00:17:02,009
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, another description of mudita, just to go back a little bit, is, eh...

218
00:17:04,468 --> 00:17:09,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And Gudrunikā again, where the Buddha talks about the three assemblies.

219
00:17:10,570 --> 00:17:10,851
[SPEAKER_01]: The...

220
00:17:10,871 --> 00:17:18,013
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it was the foremost assembly, the divided assembly and the harmonious assembly.

221
00:17:18,093 --> 00:17:31,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Harmonious assembly was monks who live regarding each other with eyes of affection, not quarreling, no disputes, with non-conflict.

222
00:17:32,629 --> 00:17:37,651
[SPEAKER_01]: That is how they live in the divine abiding of mudita.

223
00:17:38,972 --> 00:17:42,193
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's monks living together with others.

224
00:17:42,713 --> 00:17:45,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what's the best way to regard somebody with a friendly eye?

225
00:17:47,355 --> 00:17:50,496
[SPEAKER_00]: To keep visualizing and reminding yourself what a good friend that person is?

226
00:17:51,116 --> 00:17:59,600
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that depends on the circumstances, because if on another occasion they don't do something you deem as a good friend would do, means your whole friendship then is affected by it.

227
00:18:00,527 --> 00:18:02,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Means your whole state of mind would be affected by it.

228
00:18:03,208 --> 00:18:19,134
[SPEAKER_00]: But if, on the other hand, you only care about your own state of mind of friendliness, regardless of who you live with, or who comes your way, you protect your mind as you would protect a child, you would be automatically, inadvertently, incapable of ill-will towards them.

229
00:18:19,675 --> 00:18:20,675
[SPEAKER_00]: So, which is better?

230
00:18:21,195 --> 00:18:28,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Circumstantially, not being affected by ill-will, or becoming unable to be affected by ill-will?

231
00:18:30,325 --> 00:18:31,146
[SPEAKER_00]: The answer is obvious.

232
00:18:32,006 --> 00:18:42,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the best way to regard yourself with affection and regard others that you live with, with a friendly affection and eyes, is basically protect your own mind from ill will, from resistance, in regard to how you feel.

233
00:18:43,091 --> 00:18:45,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have an ill will towards a person?

234
00:18:46,835 --> 00:18:52,740
[SPEAKER_00]: that said something disagreeable to you, if you don't mind experiencing disagreeable feeling.

235
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:55,562
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable to hate that person.

236
00:18:55,582 --> 00:18:56,403
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter what they're doing.

237
00:18:56,603 --> 00:18:58,905
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would not need to deal with anything either.

238
00:18:59,445 --> 00:18:59,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

239
00:18:59,966 --> 00:19:02,928
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to maintain that, because the framework remained unaffected.

240
00:19:03,028 --> 00:19:04,949
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to look at the positive points of the person.

241
00:19:05,170 --> 00:19:05,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

242
00:19:05,730 --> 00:19:08,933
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to manage them in order for you to not hate them.

243
00:19:08,953 --> 00:19:09,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

244
00:19:10,983 --> 00:19:18,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply by not resisting the disagreeable feeling that you're experiencing, you cannot hate whoever was perceived to be the cause of that disagreeable feeling.

245
00:19:18,867 --> 00:19:19,787
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already metta.

246
00:19:20,107 --> 00:19:20,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Done properly.

247
00:19:22,228 --> 00:19:31,313
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not... you're not... you're covering all circumstances that might arise... You're basically finding like a... like a... a gateway.

248
00:19:32,239 --> 00:19:34,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Through which circumstances have to arise.

249
00:19:34,820 --> 00:19:42,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And instead of chasing and managing and dealing with preventing circumstances, you realize all I need to do is protect the gateway and not lose the sight of it.

250
00:19:43,162 --> 00:19:44,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, whatever's in, I know it's in.

251
00:19:44,423 --> 00:19:45,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever's out, I know it's out.

252
00:19:45,563 --> 00:19:46,703
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all you need to know.

253
00:19:47,884 --> 00:19:48,824
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you protect it.

254
00:19:49,484 --> 00:19:58,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Finding the context that is universal to good or bad, agreeable or disagreeable, content or discontent, and then protect that context.

255
00:20:00,207 --> 00:20:02,208
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you go beyond content and discontent.

256
00:20:06,689 --> 00:20:10,710
[SPEAKER_00]: And that context is not resisting what you're currently feeling.

257
00:20:10,750 --> 00:20:14,011
[SPEAKER_00]: And you always have to be feeling something.

258
00:20:14,151 --> 00:20:15,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Peace, peacefulness... Well, no.

259
00:20:16,272 --> 00:20:22,074
[SPEAKER_00]: You will... Peacefulness will come as a result of it, as a culmination of your absence of your resistance towards what you feel.

260
00:20:22,234 --> 00:20:22,854
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the peace.

261
00:20:22,974 --> 00:20:26,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Peace is not... not feeling the pain.

262
00:20:28,106 --> 00:20:31,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Peace is not being disturbed by the pain or not being pulled by the pleasure.

263
00:20:32,029 --> 00:20:33,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's non-conflict.

264
00:20:34,411 --> 00:20:35,151
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean by peace.

265
00:20:35,171 --> 00:20:36,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, non-conflict internally, exactly.

266
00:20:37,353 --> 00:20:38,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that's another thing.

267
00:20:38,714 --> 00:20:46,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have conflict with anybody outside, without you already being conflicted with what you're feeling at the time that you're refusing to accept?

268
00:20:47,542 --> 00:20:48,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

269
00:20:48,682 --> 00:20:52,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you remove the conflict, you can spend the whole life, dedicated whole life,

270
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:59,442
[SPEAKER_00]: In trying to eradicate the conflict out there in the world, and you will fail, because that's not where the conflict is rooted.

271
00:21:00,382 --> 00:21:07,404
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in... in basically how you... however every single individual is feeling at a time, they're resisting it or giving in to it.

272
00:21:07,985 --> 00:21:09,665
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already the root of the conflict.

273
00:21:10,685 --> 00:21:18,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you remove that internal conflict, it will be impossible to be conflicted, even if the whole world is seeking conflict with you.

274
00:21:21,693 --> 00:21:23,831
[SPEAKER_00]: To the point of, like, physically harming yourself.

